window.docContents[11].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Overloading-and-Type-Classes--Summary--Coercions":{"contents":"Coercions allow Lean to recover from what would normally be a compile-time error by inserting a call to a function that transforms data from one type to another.\nFor example, the coercion from any type  to the type  allows values to be written directly, rather than with the  constructor, making  work more like nullable types from object-oriented languages.\n\nThere are multiple kinds of coercion.\nThey can recover from different kinds of errors, and they are represented by their own type classes.\nThe  class is used to recover from type errors.\nWhen Lean has an expression of type  in a context that expects something with type , Lean first attempts to string together a chain of coercions that can transform s into s, and only displays the error when this cannot be done.\nThe  class takes the specific value being coerced as an extra parameter, allowing either further type class search to be done on the value or allowing constructors to be used in the instance to limit the scope of the conversion.\nThe  class intercepts what would otherwise be a “not a function” error when compiling a function application, and allows the value in the function position to be transformed into an actual function if possible.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Overloading and Type Classes\u0009Summary","header":"3.8.5. Coercions","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Overloading-and-Type-Classes--Summary--Coercions"}});